# {{init of|en|body mass index}}.
#: {{ux|en|The Federal Obesity Clinical Guidelines define underweight as a '''BMI''' of 18 or less, overweight as a '''BMI''' of 25 to 29.9 and obesity as a '''BMI''' of 30 and above.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Melissa Healy|title=Pear-shaped vs. apple trumps body mass index as a measure of risk|magazine=w:Los Angeles Times|url=https://www.latimes.com/science/sciencenow/la-sci-sn-pear-vs-apple-trumps-bmi-obesity-20151109-story.html|date=09-11-2015|passage=Even at '''BMIs''' above 30, where obesity begins, men and women whose waist-to-hip ratio identified them as more pear-shaped were less likely to die -- of any cause and of cardiovascular causes -- than those with similar '''BMIs''' who were apple-shaped.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022-08-30|author=Ricardo Ruiz-Villaverde; Lourdes Rodriguez Fernandez-Freire; Pilar Font-Ugalde; Manuel Galan-Gutierrez|title=Tildrakizumab: Efficacy, Safety and Survival in Mid-Term (52 Weeks) in Three Tertiary Hospitals in Andalucia (Spain)|journal=w:Journal of Clinical Medicine|volume=11|issue=17|doi=10.3390/jcm11175098|url=https://www.mdpi.com/2077-0383/11/17/5098/htm|page=5|passage=In the bivariate analysis carried out by subgroups, we did not find statistically significant differences (''p'' < 0.05) between bionaïve or bioexperienced patients, patients with '''BMI''' < 30 or '''BMI''' > 30 in relation to the efficacy variables (PASI and BSA) and analyzed PROs (VAS pruritus and DLQI).}}
